"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][28]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M28-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M28-L1 – Calibration, Uncertainty and Selective Prediction<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M28 – Trustworthy AI, Data Protection and Security<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory\nelective/cross-cutting<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6, M10, M17, M27.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish calibration from\ndiscrimination and assess an abstention rule in terms of risk and\ncoverage.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Calibration and decision quality<\/h2>\n<p>A model estimates the probability of a machine fault as 0.8. This\nnumber can be interpreted as a frequency-based probability only if,\namong comparable cases given that score, approximately 80% actually have\na fault in the long run. Good ranking performance does not guarantee\nthis property: a strictly monotonic transformation can retain the\nranking while distorting every predicted probability. For a binary score\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(X)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe ideal calibration condition is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[Y\\mid p(X)]=p(X)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalmost surely. With finite samples it is assessed approximately through\nscore bins.<\/p>\n<p>For a binary event, the Brier score\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^{-1}\\sum_i(p_i-y_i)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeasures squared deviations from observed outcomes. It reflects aspects\nof predictive quality beyond calibration. A reliability diagram plots\nthe mean predicted score against the observed event frequency in each\nbin; the diagonal represents ideal agreement. The Expected Calibration\nError (ECE), in the binary variant used here, is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_b(n_b/n)|\\bar p_b-\\bar y_b|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIt depends on bin choice, and apparent deviations for small groups are\nuncertain. Another commonly used measure, top-label ECE, compares\nconfidence in the predicted class against whether that class prediction\nwas correct, rather than evaluating the event score.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Calibration and selective risk in the teaching example<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001601_en.svg\"\nalt=\"The ideal diagonal and points (0.2, 0.25) and (0.8, 0.75) yield expected calibration error 0.05.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">The ideal diagonal and points (0.2, 0.25)\nand (0.8, 0.75) yield expected calibration error 0.05.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Calibration: predicted scores and observed\nfrequency.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Eight synthetic requests receive scores\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>.2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>.2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>.2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>.2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>.8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>.8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>.8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>.8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(.2,.2,.2,.2,.8,.8,.8,.8)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand binary outcomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,0,0,1,1,1,0,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBoth bins contain four cases. Their observed event frequencies are 0.25\nand 0.75, so ECE is 0.05. The illustration plots both points. A second,\nseparate classifier provides confidence scores and observed errors for\nsix decisions. Rejecting predictions with confidence below 0.8 accepts\nthree of six cases, all three correct. The empirical selective risk is 0\nand coverage is 0.5. Zero observed errors do not establish that the true\nrisk is zero.<\/p>\n<p>For an acceptance set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mo>:<\/mo><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>τ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_\\tau=\\{i:c_i\\ge\\tau\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncoverage is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=|A_\\tau|/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand selective risk is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>e<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>τ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=\\sum_{i\\in A_\\tau}e_i/|A_\\tau|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf the acceptance set is empty,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis undefined. A lower proportion of errors may entail additional manual\nwork, selective disadvantage or longer waiting times.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>p<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>[<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> y<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>ece<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(np.mean(p<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>v)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(v<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>y[p<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>v].mean()) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> v <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> np.unique(p))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>brier<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.mean((p<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>y)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>c<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.95<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.9<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.75<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.55<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> e<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>a<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>c<span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(ece,<span class=\"fl\">.05<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.isclose(brier,<span class=\"fl\">.19<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> a.mean()<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> e[a].mean()<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(ece,brier,a.mean(),e[a].mean())<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Uncertainty and recalibration<\/h2>\n<p>Temperature scaling uses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">softmax<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{softmax}(z/T)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor multiclass models with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA validation data set determines the temperature, for example by\nminimising the negative log-likelihood. When\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T&gt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndifferences between logits are flattened, while the predicted class\nremains unchanged if the ranking has a unique maximum. Isotonic\nregression is more flexible but needs more data. After changes to the\nmodel or data, recalibration, group performance and thresholds must be\nchecked again using independent data. Out-of-distribution (OOD)\ndetection, epistemic uncertainty (lack of knowledge) and aleatoric\nuncertainty (variation in observations) cannot all be represented fully\nby a single softmax score. Abstention requires an alternative decision\nprocess that is genuinely available and evaluated.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> What are selective risk and coverage when the\nthreshold is 0.6 in the second example? What does the difference from\n0.8 mean?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong> Five cases are accepted and one is wrong.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=5/6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=1/5=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nGreater coverage comes with higher observed risk. Choosing a threshold\nwould require an independent validation set, the cost of both errors and\nabstentions, and uncertainty intervals.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v70/guo17a.html\">Guo et\nal. (2017): On Calibration of Modern Neural Networks<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract and\npublication metadata consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v97/geifman19a.html\">Geifman\nund El-Yaniv (2019): SelectiveNet<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M28-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M28-L2 – Explainability: Local and Global Methods<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M28 – Trustworthy AI, Data Protection and Security<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory\nelective/cross-cutting<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6, M10, M17, M27.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish local explanations\nfrom global model descriptions and calculate exact Shapley contributions\nfor two features.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Local and global explanations<\/h2>\n<p>A prediction of high electricity consumption may arise from\ntemperature, operating time and their interaction. An explanation\ndescribes how a model produces its output; it does not automatically say\nhow an intervention in the real world would change consumption.\nIntrinsically interpretable models have structures that can be followed,\nsuch as small decision trees. Post-hoc methods approximate or decompose\nthe behaviour of an already trained model. Local explanations focus on\none input or its vicinity; global explanations summarise behaviour\nacross multiple inputs or the distribution of interest.<\/p>\n<p>LIME fits a simple surrogate model to model queries in a weighted\nneighbourhood of the point of interest. Its usefulness depends on\nneighbourhood selection and the fidelity of that local approximation.\nSHAP employs contributions drawn from cooperative game theory. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfeatures and a value function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v(S)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe Shapley value is the average marginal contribution of feature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>j<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nacross possible orders:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>⊆<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo>\\<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><\/munder><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>!<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>!<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mi>!<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo>∪<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi_j=\\sum_{S\\subseteq N\\setminus\\{j\\}}\\frac{|S|!(m-|S|-1)!}{m!}[v(S\\cup\\{j\\})-v(S)].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>How missing features are represented, such as through a reference\ndistribution, is part of the definition of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>v<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Shapley decomposition of our model<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001699_en.svg\"\nalt=\"A waterfall starts at the base value 2 and adds contributions 5 and 4 to reach prediction 11.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A waterfall starts at the base value 2\nand adds contributions 5 and 4 to reach prediction 11.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Shapley contributions to a prediction.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Consider our own small regression model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>a<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>a<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(a,b)=2+3a+b+2ab<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith baseline\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand point\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFeatures not included in a coalition are fixed to their baseline values.\nThe four coalition values are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>⌀<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v(\\varnothing)=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v(\\{a\\})=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v(\\{b\\})=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v(\\{a,b\\})=11<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe interaction amounts to 4. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>a<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe contribution is 3 without, and 7 with,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalready included; their average is 5. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>b<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe two contributions are 2 and 6, with average 4. Hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>11<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">11=2+5+4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe waterfall diagram shows the baseline and contributions. This\ndecomposition is exact for the chosen reference, not universal across\nother background distributions.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> fractions <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> Fraction <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> F<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> f(a,b): <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>a<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>b<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>a<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>b<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>v0,va,vb,vab<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>f(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>),f(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>),f(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>),f(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>pa<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>F(va<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>v0<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>vab<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>vb,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> pb<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>F(vb<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>v0<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>vab<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>va,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> (pa,pb)<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> v0<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>pa<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>pb<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>vab<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(v0,pa,pb,vab)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Global evaluation and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Global permutation importance assesses how much a test loss changes\nwhen a feature’s values are shuffled between observations. It must be\nevaluated on data not used for training. Correlated features can\nsubstitute for one another; unconditional permutations can also produce\nunrealistic combinations. Global SHAP summaries may use mean absolute\ncontributions across a defined population. A large absolute value does\nnot indicate an effect direction. Partial Dependence averages model\npredictions when a feature is fixed while varying other inputs;\nIndividual Conditional Expectation (ICE) curves do this separately for\nindividual observations. These methods too can evaluate implausible\ncombinations. Useful explanations must be checked for fidelity to the\nmodel, stability under small changes and clear communication of their\nreference. Causal conclusions need further assumptions and evidence\n(M21).<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> Calculate the contributions at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(a,b)=(1,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nusing the same baseline. Which interaction is distributed?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong> The four values are 2, 5, 3 and 8.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi_a=(3+5)/2=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi_b=(1+3)/2=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe interaction of 2 is shared equally between the features. The\nexplanation does not recommend changing a feature in the real world.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.07874\">Lundberg and Lee (2017):\nA Unified Approach to Interpreting Model Predictions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract page\nconsulted<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1602.04938\">Ribeiro, Singh and\nGuestrin (2016): Why Should I Trust You?<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract page\nconsulted<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M28-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M28-L3 – Fairness Metrics, Biases and Trade-offs<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M28 – Trustworthy AI, Data Protection and Security<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory\nelective/cross-cutting<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6, M10, M17, M27.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate group-specific\nselection, true-positive and false-positive rates and recognise\nconflicts between fairness criteria.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Group-based criteria<\/h2>\n<p>A classifier recommends additional training opportunities. Historical\ntarget labels may reflect unequal access to previous training rather\nthan actual need. Bias can arise during data collection, labelling,\nsampling, feature engineering or deployment. Even when a protected\ncharacteristic is not supplied as an input, correlated features may\nindirectly convey it. Fairness involves procedural justice, legitimate\ngoals and opportunities to challenge a decision, not just metrics. The\nfollowing groups A and B are entirely synthetic and contain no real\npersonal data.<\/p>\n<p>Demographic parity compares\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>Y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(\\hat Y=1\\mid G=g)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nequality of opportunity for positive cases compares the true-positive\nrate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>Y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(\\hat Y=1\\mid Y=1,G=g)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEqualised odds requires both this true-positive rate (TPR) and the\nfalse-positive rate (FPR),\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>Y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(\\hat Y=1\\mid Y=0,G=g)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nto be equal across groups. Positive predictive value (PPV) is the\nfraction of positive predictions that are actually positive. Each\nmeasure requires a defined denominator. When there are no positive\ncases, TPR is undefined.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two groups with equal TPR and FPR<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001571_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Both groups have TPR 75% and FPR 10%; selection rates 36% and 23%, and PPV approximately 83% and 65%.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Both groups have TPR 75% and FPR 10%;\nselection rates 36% and 23%, and PPV approximately 83% and\n65%.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Equal error rates but unequal selection.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Group A contains 100 cases with 40 positive target values. Group B\nalso has 100 cases but only 20 positive target values. Both classifiers\nachieve TPR = 0.75 and FPR = 0.10. In A, this gives 30 true and 6 false\npositives; in B, 15 true and 8 false positives. The selection rates are\n0.36 and 0.23, while PPV values are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>30<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>36<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.833<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">30/36\\approx0.833<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>15<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>23<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.652<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">15/23\\approx0.652<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram illustrates why equal conditional error rates do not\nnecessarily imply equal selection rates or precision.<\/p>\n<p>With prevalence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_g=P(Y=1\\mid G=g)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nBayes’ rule gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{PPV}_g=\\frac{\\mathrm{TPR}_g\\pi_g}{\\mathrm{TPR}_g\\pi_g+\\mathrm{FPR}_g(1-\\pi_g)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With equal positive TPR and FPR but different prevalence, PPV\ngenerally differs. Exceptional cases, such as perfect classification,\nrequire separate consideration.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> fractions <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> Fraction <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> F<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> metrics(tp,fp,fn,tn):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> F(tp,tp<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>fn),F(fp,fp<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>tn),F(tp<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>fp,tp<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>fp<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>fn<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>tn),F(tp,tp<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>fp)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>a<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>metrics(<span class=\"dv\">30<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">54<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> b<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>metrics(<span class=\"dv\">15<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">72<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> a[:<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>b[:<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>(F(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>),F(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> a[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>:]<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>(F(<span class=\"dv\">36<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>),F(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>)) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> b[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>:]<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>(F(<span class=\"dv\">23<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">100<\/span>),F(<span class=\"dv\">15<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">23<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(a,b)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Audits and trade-offs<\/h2>\n<p>Before an audit, explain why the target, group definitions and\ncomparison measure are suitable. Report error rates alongside sample\ncounts, statistical uncertainty and, where appropriate, intersectional\ngroups. Small groups require particular care; merely combining them may\nhide disadvantage. Possible measures include better data collection and\nlabel review, constraints in training, and adjustments to downstream\ndecisions. Group-specific thresholds may raise legal or ethical problems\nand require separate review. Decisions about trade-offs should not be\nleft solely to an optimisation algorithm. A fairness metric proves\nneither the absence of discrimination nor legal compliance in\ngeneral.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> Suppose group B now has 40 positive and 60\nnegative cases, while the conditional rates remain unchanged. What are\nits selection rate and PPV?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong> There are 30 true and 6 false positives,\ngiving a 36% selection rate and a PPV of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5/6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the original teaching example, the difference at fixed conditional\nrates resulted from different prevalence. The arithmetic cannot tell us\nwhether that prevalence difference is real, caused by measurement error\nor due to systemic disadvantage.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.02413\">Hardt, Price and Srebro\n(2016): Equality of Opportunity in Supervised Learning<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · article\npage consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.07524\">Chouldechova (2017): Fair\nPrediction with Disparate Impact<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract page consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M28-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M28-L4 – Data Protection Techniques and Differential Privacy<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M28 – Trustworthy AI, Data Protection and Security<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory\nelective/cross-cutting<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6, M10, M17, M27.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Formulate a formal privacy\nguarantee and choose the Laplace scale for a count query.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Privacy as a formal property<\/h2>\n<p>Removing names does not necessarily anonymise a data set. Timestamps,\nrare features and combinations with other sources may allow\nre-identification. Pseudonymisation separates identities from working\ndata, but is generally reversible when the linking information exists.\nAccess controls, encryption, data minimisation and deletion policies\naddress different risks. Differential Privacy (DP) adds a mathematical\nguarantee for the output of a randomised mechanism.<\/p>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>D<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D,D&#39;<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe neighbouring data sets which, in this example, differ by the addition\nor removal of one person contributing one record. For all measurable\nsets of outputs\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\varepsilon,\\delta)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-DP\nrequires:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∈<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><\/msup><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>M<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>D<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∈<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>δ<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(M(D)\\in S)\\le e^\\varepsilon P(M(D&#39;)\\in S)+\\delta.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Pure DP uses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA smaller\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ε<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nlimits one person’s influence more strictly. It is not a percentage of\nprivacy. The definition of neighbouring data sets must match the entity\nbeing protected; when one person contributes multiple records, their\ntotal contribution must be bounded at person level. DP does not promise\nthat no inference about a person is possible; it limits the additional\ninfluence of their participation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Laplace mechanism for a count query<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001620_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Two overlapping Laplace probability densities centered at counts 40 and 41, both with scale 2.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two overlapping Laplace probability\ndensities centered at counts 40 and 41, both with scale 2.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Laplace noise around adjacent counts.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A count query\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncounts at most one contribution per person. Its global\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>L<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsensitivity is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta q=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Laplace mechanism releases\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(D)+Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>∼<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z\\sim\\mathrm{Lap}(0,b)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=\\Delta q/\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe density is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(z)=\\exp(-|z|/b)/(2b)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the standard deviation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mi>b<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.83<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{2}b\\approx2.83<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe triangle inequality bounds the density ratio of two neighbouring\noutput distributions by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exp(\\Delta q/b)=e^\\varepsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe plot shows densities for true counts 40 and 41; their extensive\noverlap illustrates, but does not prove, the guarantee.<\/p>\n<p>The probability that absolute noise is no greater than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-e^{-t/b}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis is approximately 86.47%. The released value can be non-integer or\nnegative. Subsequent rounding or clipping to an allowed range without\nfurther access to raw data retains the DP guarantee, but may introduce\nbias.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> math<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>b<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>p<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>math.exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span>b)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> density(x,q): <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> math.exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(x<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>q)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>b)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>b)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>ratios<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[density(x<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">40<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>density(x<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">41<\/span>) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">300<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">501<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(ratios)<span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span>math.exp(<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1e-12<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> math.isclose(p,<span class=\"fl\">.8646647167633873<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(b, math.sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>b,p,<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(ratios))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Composition and practical limitations<\/h2>\n<p>When the same protected population is queried repeatedly, privacy\nbudgets must be accounted for. The simple sequential composition bound\nadds the respective epsilon and delta values. More advanced accounting\ntechniques can provide tighter bounds under suitable assumptions.\nPost-processing that does not access the raw data again uses no further\nbudget. DP-SGD clips individual gradient contributions and adds noise;\nits actual guarantee depends, among other factors, on sampling,\nclipping, noise scale and number of training steps. Production\nmechanisms require vetted libraries, secure randomness, carefully\nenforced contribution bounds and protection against side channels. The\ncode above checks distribution values; it is not a DP release service.\nDP alone ensures neither legal compliance nor comprehensive data\nsecurity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> Three queries each use pure DP with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon=0.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non the same population. What does simple composition imply? What scale\nis needed for one count query with at most two contributions per person\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon=0.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong> The total guarantee is at most\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon=0.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\delta=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder add/remove-one-person adjacency, sensitivity is at most 2 and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.3<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>6.67<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=2/0.3\\approx6.67<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA bound per row does not replace the required bound per person.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.cis.upenn.edu/~aaroth/privacybook.html\">Dwork\nand Roth (2014): The Algorithmic Foundations of Differential Privacy<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· book overview consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj\">EU:\nRegulation (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · direct retrieval blocked by\nJavaScript check<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M28-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M28-L5 – Federated Learning and Confidential Data Processing<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M28 – Trustworthy AI, Data Protection and Security<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory\nelective/cross-cutting<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6, M10, M17, M27.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate federated model\naggregation and explain the limits of data locality and secure\naggregation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Distributed training<\/h2>\n<p>Three workshops wish to improve a prediction model jointly without\ntransferring their complete measurement logs to a central location. In\nhorizontal federated learning, participants have similar feature schemas\nbut different observations. A coordinator distributes shared model\nparameters, selected participants train locally, send updates back and\nthe coordinator aggregates them. Vertical federated learning instead\ninvolves different feature sets for overlapping entities, requiring\nadditional matching and protection mechanisms.<\/p>\n<p>The objective for weighted empirical risk minimisation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F(w)=\\sum_k(n_k/N)F_k(w)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=\\sum_k n_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEach local loss\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis averaged over its own data set. After local training steps, FedAvg\naggregates the local models\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>w<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w^{t+1}=\\sum_k(n_k/N)w_k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFollowing a single common gradient descent step, this weighting matches\nthe central gradient on the participating data; after several local\nsteps the results need not be identical. Non-identically distributed\ndata (non-IID), participant drop-outs and varying computational capacity\naffect convergence and representation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>FedAvg and masked summation<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001575_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Weighted local contributions 0.2, 0.6, and 2.0 sum to 2.8.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Weighted local contributions 0.2, 0.6,\nand 2.0 sum to 2.8.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Weighted averaging of local model updates.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>In the teaching example, sites with 20, 30 and 50 observations return\nscalar local model parameters 1, 2 and 4. The unweighted average would\nbe\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>7<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.333<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7/3\\approx2.333<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nFedAvg instead gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>200<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(20+60+200)/100=2.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe plot shows each site’s contribution to the weighted sum. Weighting\nby sample size corresponds to the stated objective but places more\nemphasis on larger sites; if every site is to carry equal importance, a\ndifferent objective is possible.<\/p>\n<p>With secure aggregation, the coordinator should learn only the\naggregate, not individual updates. A simplified arithmetic illustration\nuses pairwise cancelling masks: when participants add masks to their\ncontributions and all masks cancel in the sum, the aggregate is\nrecovered. Real protocols also require a precise cryptographic security\ndefinition, key management and resilience to drop-outs. The code below\nsimulates only algebraic cancellation, not a secure protocol.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> fractions <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> Fraction <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> F<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>n<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[<span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">30<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">50<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> w<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> s<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[a<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>b <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> a,b <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">zip<\/span>(n,w)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>m01,m02,m12<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">17<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">31<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">9<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>masked<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[s[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>m01<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>m02,s[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>m01<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>m12,s[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>m02<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>m12]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(masked)<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(s)<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">280<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> F(<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(s),<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(n))<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>F(<span class=\"dv\">14<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(s,masked,F(<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(masked),<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(n)))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Confidential processing and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Even if raw data remain local, updates can leak information;\nmalicious participants can also corrupt a model by submitting unsuitable\nupdates. Secure aggregation protects individual contributions under the\nassumptions of its protocol, but cannot prevent every inference from a\npublished aggregate. DP can additionally limit the influence of\nindividual participants or records. Encryption during storage and\ntransfer protects other processing stages. Trusted execution\nenvironments (TEEs) aim to isolate data while they are processed,\nsubject to hardware and side-channel assumptions. Secure multiparty\ncomputation and homomorphic encryption permit certain computations\nwithout exposing plaintext, often with additional processing and\ncommunication costs. Architectural choices must follow the threat model,\ndata distributions, protection goals and expected failure\nconditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> Site C drops out before any exchange. A and B\nare aggregated alone using newly agreed masks and weights. What\nparameter results, and why is simply deleting the third masked value\nfrom the original sum insufficient?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>30<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(20+60)/(20+30)=1.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith the original masking scheme, masks for pairs involving C would no\nlonger cancel. A real protocol must handle drop-outs under its security\nassumptions; simply dropping one masked contribution produces the wrong\naggregate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://proceedings.mlr.press/v54/mcmahan17a.html\">McMahan\net al. (2017): Communication-Efficient Learning of Deep Networks from\nDecentralized Data<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract page consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://research.google/pubs/practical-secure-aggregation-for-privacy-preserving-machine-learning/\">Bonawitz\net al. (2017): Practical Secure Aggregation for Privacy-Preserving\nMachine Learning<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.cis.upenn.edu/~aaroth/privacybook.html\">Dwork\nand Roth (2014): The Algorithmic Foundations of Differential Privacy<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· book overview consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M28-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M28-L6 – Threat Models, Robustness and Red Teaming<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M28 – Trustworthy AI, Data Protection and Security<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory\nelective/cross-cutting<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6, M10, M17, M27.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Classify threats across the\nmodel lifecycle and calculate robustness within a bounded synthetic test\nmodel.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Threat models and robustness<\/h2>\n<p>A model tested before delivery may later become unreliable because of\nunusual data, errors or deliberate manipulation. A threat model\nidentifies assets to protect (such as decision integrity,\nconfidentiality or service availability), exposed interfaces, the\nassumed capabilities and knowledge of an attacker, and the permitted\nscope of testing. Test conclusions are meaningful only under these\nassumptions. During training, tampered data or model updates can\nundermine integrity. During inference, deliberately altered yet\nplausible inputs may trigger errors. Information leakage and model\nextraction concern confidentiality and intellectual property. With\ngenerative systems, manipulated external content and unintended tool\nactions present additional system risks.<\/p>\n<p>A robustness measurement requires a well-defined set of permitted\ninput variations. Local robustness for a classifier can mean that the\npredicted class is unchanged for every\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̃<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tilde x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin a specified neighbourhood of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEmpirical tests provide evidence against tested perturbations. Formal\ncertification covers only the precisely defined perturbation set and\nmust account for implementation limitations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Robustness interval for a linear decision<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001694_en.svg\"\nalt=\"A robust score interval extends from 0.5 to 3.5; a broader interval from −1 to 5 crosses the zero decision boundary.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A robust score interval extends from 0.5\nto 3.5; a broader interval from −1 to 5 crosses the zero decision\nboundary.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Score robustness near a linear decision\nboundary.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A synthetic quality-inspection system uses a linear score\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s(x)=2x_1-x_2-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe positive class is selected when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=(2,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe score is 2. Sensor errors are modelled by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\|\\Delta x\\|_\\infty\\le r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msup><mi>w<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|w^T\\Delta x|\\le\\|w\\|_1 r=3r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe possible score lies in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>r<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>r<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[2-3r,2+3r]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe lower bound is 0.5 and the decision stays positive. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe interval extends from -1 to 5 and contains both classes. The strict\npositive-score guarantee applies when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r&lt;2/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nat exactly\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe class is still positive under the stated tie rule. The diagram shows\nthe score intervals and threshold. In real data, admissible disturbances\nare often more complicated than a norm ball.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> itertools <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> product<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> fractions <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> Fraction <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> F<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> r,expected <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> [(F(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>),(F(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>),F(<span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>))), (F(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>),(F(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>),F(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>)))]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    scores<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>a)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>b)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> a,b <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> product([<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>r,r],repeat<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> (<span class=\"bu\">min<\/span>(scores),<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(scores))<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>expected<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(r,<span class=\"bu\">min<\/span>(scores),<span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(scores))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Authorised security testing and safeguards<\/h2>\n<p>An authorised red team develops tests from realistic risk scenarios,\nworks in an isolated environment with dummy data and agreed boundaries,\nand records findings by reproducibility, impact and severity. Risks\nshould be reduced by design where feasible: protect the provenance and\nintegrity of training data, minimise permissions, separate untrusted\ncontent from instructions, validate outputs, limit tool permissions and\ntest fallbacks and rollback paths. Adversarial training may improve\nrobustness to specified perturbations but cannot guarantee unlimited\nsecurity. A red-team exercise is not permission to test third-party\nsystems. After fixes, individual findings are reproduced and regression\ntests added; monitoring observes residual risks in operation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> For the same model, the initial score is only\n0.3. What is the maximum perturbation radius that guarantees a\nnon-negative score under the same assumptions?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong> From\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.3<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>r<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.3-3r\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r\\le0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA change in input scaling or in the threat model requires a new\ncalculation. A robustness guarantee does not necessarily imply correct\nclassification: a consistently wrong decision is still wrong.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.nist.gov/publications/adversarial-machine-learning-taxonomy-and-terminology-attacks-and-mitigations-0\">Vassilev\net al. (2025): Adversarial Machine Learning: A Taxonomy and Terminology\nof Attacks and Mitigations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · NIST publication page consulted<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\">NIST\n(2023): Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework 1.0<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · PDF\ntable of contents and p. 20 (PDF p. 25) inspected<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M28-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M28-L7 – Risk Management, Governance and Legal Responsibility<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M28 – Trustworthy AI, Data Protection and Security<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory\nelective/cross-cutting<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6, M10, M17, M27.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Create an auditable risk\nregister, prioritise controls and distinguish organisational from legal\nobligations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Governance and risk registers<\/h2>\n<p>An image recognition system assists the visual inspection of\nmanufactured components. False approvals and unnecessary rejection\nproduce different harms; potential data protection violations, downtime\nand consequences of incorrect work instructions must also be considered.\nRisks arise from the interaction of the model, interfaces, users, data\nand deployment environment. Governance allocates accountability,\ndecision-making powers, controls, documentation and escalation routes. A\nrisk register should identify at least the hazard, triggering\nconditions, affected parties, impact, existing controls, accountable\nrole, supporting evidence, residual risk and next review date. Approval\nfor deployment must be tied to defined acceptance conditions.<\/p>\n<p>The voluntary NIST AI RMF 1.0 organises such activities into Govern,\nMap, Measure and Manage. Govern addresses organisational foundations,\nMap the deployment context, Measure risk assessment, and Manage the\ntreatment of identified risks. These functions are not a rigid sequence\nand do not replace legal review. A technical benchmark alone cannot\ncapture the consequences of deploying a system.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Expected costs and residual risks<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001688_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Without controls expected losses are 16000 euros; with controls expected losses are 4000 euros plus 3000 euros in control costs.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Without controls expected losses are\n16000 euros; with controls expected losses are 4000 euros plus 3000\neuros in control costs.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Expected monthly risk with and without control.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For a synthetic inspection line, suppose 1,000 components are checked\neach month. Before an additional control, 2% are incorrectly approved\ndespite being defective; after that control, 0.5% do. For this example,\nassume a fixed loss of €800 for every such approval. Expected monthly\nloss before the control is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1000<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>800<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16000<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1000\\cdot0.02\\cdot800=16000<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\neuros, and after it\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1000<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.005<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>800<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4000<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1000\\cdot0.005\\cdot800=4000<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\neuros. The extra control costs €3,000 a month, so the expected financial\nsaving is €9,000. The graph shows expected losses and control costs.\nThis calculation assumes constant volume, error rates and consequences;\nit does not estimate real-world damage.<\/p>\n<p>More generally, for a suitable decomposition into discrete loss\nevents,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=\\sum_i p_i c_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the expected value of a random loss. Rights that cannot be monetised,\nextremely rare severe outcomes, accumulating harms and uncertainty\nrequire additional qualitative and scenario-based assessment. An\nexpected financial saving is not sufficient on its own to approve\ndeployment.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> decimal <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> Decimal <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> D<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>n<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>D(<span class=\"dv\">1000<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> cost<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>D(<span class=\"dv\">800<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> control<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>D(<span class=\"dv\">3000<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>old<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>n<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>D(<span class=\"st\">&#39;.02&#39;<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>cost<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> new<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>n<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>D(<span class=\"st\">&#39;.005&#39;<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>cost<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>saving<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>old<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>new<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>control<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> (old,new,saving)<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>(D(<span class=\"dv\">16000<\/span>),D(<span class=\"dv\">4000<\/span>),D(<span class=\"dv\">9000<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(old,new,control,saving)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Legal framework and scope of review<\/h2>\n<p>The EU AI Act (Regulation (EU) 2024/1689) assigns requirements to\ncertain systems and organisational roles. Article 9, as consulted\nthrough the European Commission’s AI Act Service Desk, requires a\ndocumented risk-management system for high-risk AI systems, maintained\nand periodically reviewed across the lifecycle. This includes\nforeseeable hazards and misuse, suitable safeguards and testing. Article\n14 addresses human oversight; additional obligations depend on the\nsystem’s actual classification. A data protection assessment is\ndistinct. Whether personal data are processed, which legal basis applies\nand what obligations arise must be assessed for the particular use under\nthe GDPR and other applicable laws. The EUR-Lex texts were inaccessible\nin direct retrieval; the AI Act articles described here came from the\nCommission’s explorer (consolidated version indicated there as 27 July\n2026). No statement is made about current dates of application, and no\ndefinitive legal review has been performed. Before real-world\ndeployment, the official texts, transitional provisions and\nclassification must be checked separately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> Suppose the control now costs €13,000 a month.\nHow does the expected financial result change? May the control be\nomitted for that reason alone?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>16000<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4000<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>13000<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1000<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">16000-4000-13000=-1000<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\neuros. Under the assumptions made, the financial teaching indicator\nargues against the control. It does not imply that the control is\nunnecessary: safety requirements, affected people’s rights, uncertainty,\nalternatives and regulatory duties must be assessed independently.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\">NIST\n(2023): Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework 1.0<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · PDF\ntable of contents and p. 20 (PDF p. 25) inspected<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-9\">EU\nAI Act Service Desk: Article 9 – Risk management system<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · article\ntext consulted, explorer states consolidated version dated\n2026-07-27<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-14\">EU\nAI Act Service Desk: Article 14 – Human oversight<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · article text\nconsulted, explorer states consolidated version dated 2026-07-27<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj\">EU:\nRegulation (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · direct retrieval blocked by\nJavaScript check<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689\">EU: Regulation\n(EU) 2024/1689 (AI Act)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · direct retrieval blocked by JavaScript\ncheck<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M28-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M28-L8 – Human Oversight, Interaction and Impact Assessment<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M28 – Trustworthy AI, Data Protection and Security<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory\nelective/cross-cutting<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M6, M10, M17, M27.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Design human oversight as a\ntestable process and assess outcomes using error rates, intervention\ncapacity and complaint routes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Effective human oversight<\/h2>\n<p>An assistance system ranks maintenance requests by urgency. Employees\nmay follow or change its recommendations. Effective oversight depends on\navailable time, expertise, access to inputs, incentives and actual\nauthority. A confirmation button by itself does not establish\nindependent judgement. Automation bias is the tendency to follow an\nautomated recommendation despite contrary evidence. Conversely, too many\nwarnings can cause important alerts to be missed. Interfaces should\nappropriately show uncertainty, limits of applicability, the relevant\ninputs and feasible alternatives, avoiding unwarranted precision.<\/p>\n<p>Human-in-the-loop means that a person intervenes within the decision\npathway; human-on-the-loop means a supervisory role with the ability to\nintervene. The appropriate arrangement depends on potential harm,\navailable response time and the real ability to act. Escalation,\nstopping and returning to a safe process must be planned, tested and\nadequately staffed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Capacity of a manual review process<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001634_en.svg\"\nalt=\"The backlog grows from zero to 80 cases over five days at a rate of 16 additional cases per day.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">The backlog grows from zero to 80 cases\nover five days at a rate of 16 additional cases per day.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Manual review backlog and capacity.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A synthetic service handles 200 cases each day, of which 30% are\nreferred for manual review under a predefined rule: 60 cases. Two\nreviewers each have 180 minutes of net review time, and each case takes\nexactly eight minutes in the simplified model. With sequential\nprocessing, each person can complete at most 22 cases per day, or 44\ntogether. At a constant arrival rate, the backlog therefore grows by 16\ncases each day. The chart illustrates why an abstention rule without\nsufficient review capacity may fail as a safety measure. The theoretical\nminimum number of reviewers is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⌈<\/mo><mn>60<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>22<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⌉<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lceil60/22\\rceil=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFluctuations, breaks, complex cases and time-critical decisions may\nrequire further capacity.<\/p>\n<p>A second test should compare decision quality with and without\nassistance using separately allocated test cases, for example errors,\nprocessing times, override rates and missed model errors. The number of\nchanged recommendations is neither proof of quality nor an unambiguous\nindicator of errors.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> math <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> ceil<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>incoming<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">200<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">30<\/span><span class=\"op\">//<\/span><span class=\"dv\">100<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> per_reviewer<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">180<\/span><span class=\"op\">//<\/span><span class=\"dv\">8<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> capacity<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>per_reviewer<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>backlog<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[d<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(incoming<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>capacity) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> d <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> (incoming,capacity,backlog[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],ceil(incoming<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>per_reviewer))<span class=\"op\">==<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">60<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">44<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">80<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(incoming,capacity,backlog,ceil(incoming<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>per_reviewer))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Impact assessment and feedback<\/h2>\n<p>An impact assessment describes purpose, limitations, affected people,\npotential harms, their distribution, mitigations, responsibilities and\nmonitoring indicators. Participation by those affected can reveal\nproblems that laboratory tests miss. Accessible complaint and correction\nprocesses must remain available even when the system fails. Changes in\nthe deployment context or model require reassessment. According to\nArticle 14 of the EU AI Act as read through the Commission’s Service\nDesk, appropriate human oversight capabilities should be available for\nhigh-risk systems. Article 27 concerns a fundamental-rights impact\nassessment for certain deployers and contexts, not for every AI\napplication. Legal applicability and the relationship to data protection\nimpact assessments must be checked against the official texts; direct\nretrieval from EUR-Lex was blocked. No legal classification is made for\nthis teaching example.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Task:<\/strong> One more reviewer with 180 minutes of net\nreview time joins the team. Can the backlog of 80 cases be eliminated if\nthe new arrival rate stays unchanged?<\/p>\n<p><strong>Solution:<\/strong> Capacity increases to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>22<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>66<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\cdot22=66<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncases per day. Six outstanding cases can thus be cleared each day,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⌈<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⌉<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>14<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lceil80/6\\rceil=14<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndays. The model assumes fixed review times and continuous availability.\nReal planning must consider urgency, overload, errors under time\npressure and response deadlines.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.100-1.pdf\">NIST\n(2023): Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework 1.0<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · PDF\ntable of contents and p. 20 (PDF p. 25) inspected<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-14\">EU\nAI Act Service Desk: Article 14 – Human oversight<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · article text\nconsulted, explorer states consolidated version dated 2026-07-27<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ai-act-service-desk.ec.europa.eu/en/ai-act/article-27\">EU\nAI Act Service Desk: Article 27 – Fundamental rights impact\nassessment<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · article text consulted, explorer states consolidated\nversion dated 2026-07-27<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2016/679/oj\">EU:\nRegulation (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · direct retrieval blocked by\nJavaScript check<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1689\">EU: Regulation\n(EU) 2024/1689 (AI Act)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · direct retrieval blocked by JavaScript\ncheck<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
